{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779594282,"nonce":"uy/WD1V/2pz28OPgcrZSd+QooW8=","title":"Studio analyses should request observable inputs","message":"## Problem\nExplicit retained observables are an authored data-capture surface, but analysis/evaluation views also need to consume graph variables. The retention/materialization requirements for an analysis input should belong to the analysis request, not to a naked observable row.\n\n## Contract\n- Analysis input pickers should offer the same canonical selector space as objectives and retained observables: graph ports, graph edges, graph outputs, recurrent carries, state paths, and task data where available.\n- If an analysis uses a selector that is not explicitly retained by the user, the analysis plan should add it as an implicit required observable for that run or artifact.\n- Retention/materialization details such as full trajectory, rolling window, or streaming aggregation should be specified by the analysis term or artifact plan that consumes the selector.\n- Explicit retained observables remain useful as always-captured trajectories users want to inspect, compare, export, or reuse across analyses.\n\n## Related Work\n- Core graph/retained-observable contract: 07c0ec9.\n- Explicit observable authoring UI: d72b09a.\n- Provider eval/analysis artifact manifests: 63c798f.\n- Broader analysis migration: e8452a4.\n\n## Done Criteria\n- Analysis UI can select from canonical graph/task/state variables even when they are not pre-authored retained observables.\n- Lowering merges analysis-required observables with explicit retained observables and objective-required observables.\n- Manifest/artifact output distinguishes explicit captures from analysis-required inputs.\n- The UI does not ask users to choose retention for naked observables; consumer plans own materialization.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}